ree line, and the path connecting home to the unknown forest.\n\n### 3. The Forest Threshold â\200\224 18,000 to 27,000 ms\n\nThe forest changes scale around Red. The path narrows, light becomes patterned, and ordinary branches begin to resemble watchful forms. Red continues walking; the environment must be alive within the shot.\n\n### 4. The Wolf Appears â\200\224 27,000 to 38,000 ms\n\nThe wolf emerges gradually rather than popping into frame. Stage a real encounter with eyelines and shot/reverse-shot logic. He is charming, controlled, and unsettling rather than immediately monstrous.\n\nRequired line: **â\200\234Good morning, little traveler. Where does this path take you?â\200\235**\n\n### 5. The Question â\200\224 38,000 to 47,000 ms\n\nRed reveals that she is visiting her grandmother at the cottage beneath three old oak trees. The wolf notices the basket, studies the route, and directs Red\'s attention toward wildflowers away from the path.\n\n### 6. The Race â\200\224 47,000 to 57,000 ms\n\nIntercut two simultaneous actions: Red gathering flowers in a bright clearing, and the wolf racing through a darker shortcut. The cutting rhythm must accelerate while preserving where each character is going.\n\n### 7. At Grandmother\'s Door â\200\224 57,000 to 66,000 ms\n\nThe wolf reaches the cottage, imitates Red\'s knock, and enters. Show the danger through framing, shadows, objects, and reactionâ\200\224not gore. Grandmother is trapped out of sight but remains alive.\n\n### 8. The Disguise â\200\224 66,000 to 75,000 ms\n\nInside the cottage, the wolf constructs the disguise using Grandmother\'s nightcap, spectacles, quilt, and bed. Cross-cut with Red approaching through the trees so the audience understands the trap before she does.\n\n### 9. Red Arrives â\200\224 75,000 to 84,000 ms\n\nRed enters the unnaturally quiet cottage. The basket, flowers, half-open door, bed curtain, and altered quality of light should build suspense. She approaches the bed.\n\n### 10. What