ionships.\n\n## Cinematic and illustration requirements\n\n- Give every sequence a distinct full-frame composition; do not reuse one stage with palette swaps.\n- Use at least four clearly different shot scales across the film: extreme wide, wide, medium, close-up, or extreme close-up.\n- Use motivated camera behavior such as tracking, push-in, pan, parallax, rack-like focus, or handheld tension. Do not simply zoom the entire page.\n- Include at least one match cut, one reveal motivated by occlusion or light, and the required parallel editing during **The Race**.\n- Animate within shots: walking cycles, breathing, fabric, hair, foliage, weather, shadows, gaze, hands, props, and environmental response. A crossfade between static illustrations is not sufficient.\n- Maintain screen direction, eyelines, entrances, exits, and geography so the film can be understood without captions.\n- Establish a coherent light arc: warm village morning, uncertain forest, cold cottage danger, and changed dawn.\n- Use a recurring visual motif that evolves across the whole filmâ\200\224for example a red thread, path curve, leaf shape, basket weave, or another authored idea.\n- Typography and captions must be integrated into the film\'s visual language, not placed in generic UI cards.\n- Keep prose beyond the required lines minimal.\n- Take a specific art-direction position. Avoid generic gradient backgrounds, stock dashboard aesthetics, emoji, clip-art shorthand, and interchangeable flat vector scenes.\n\n## Playback experience\n\n- Provide Play/Pause, Replay, 0.5Ã\227/1Ã\227/2Ã\227 speed, captions toggle, and a scrubber with 12 clickable sequence markers.\n- Controls must be discreet, keyboard accessible, touch friendly, and fade visually when unused without becoming impossible to recover.\n- Support Space for Play/Pause, Left/Right for a short seek, and Home for replay.\n- Any manual timeline action may take over from autoplay, but deterministic playback must remain available.\n- Replay must restore seed `1812`, time 0, autoplay, and all visual state.\n- Adapt composition to desktop and narrow mobile viewports without cropping away essential action.\n- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion` by simplifying camera motion and transitions while preserving the complete 112-second narrative and exact sequence boundaries.\n- Keep the console free of avoidable errors.\n\n## Determinism\n\nAll story state, visual variation, motion, cuts, particles, weather, and procedural illustration must be reproducible from a supplied seed and exact timeline time. Animation must derive from the deterministic film clock, not accumulated display refresh frames.\n\nUse fixed-step or absolute-time evaluation, cap wall-clock deltas, and handle tab visibility changes without skipping story state. Seeking to the same millisecond with the same seed and viewport must reproduce the same meaningful scene state.\n\n## Deterministic film interface\n\nExpose `window.__RED_HOOD_FILM__` with callable methods:\n\n- `reset(seed)` â\200\224 restore time 0 and all film state with the supplied integer seed;\n- `play()` â\200\224 continue deterministic playback;\n- `pause()` â\200\224 stop film-time advancement;\n- `seek(milliseconds)` â\200\224 render the exact state from 0 through 112000;\n- `seekScene(index)` â\200\224 seek to the start of a sequence using zero-based indices 0 through 11;\n- `setSpeed(multiplier)` â\200\224 accept 0.5, 1, or 2;\n- `setCaptions(enabled)` â\200\224 show or hide captions without changing film state;\n- `getShotList()` â\200\224 return the 12 sequence names, indices, start times, and end times;\n- `snapshot()` â\200\224 return a JSON-safe summary containing seed, playing, speed, film time, duration, sequence index, sequence name, progress, captions state, completion state, and meaningful character/scene state;\n- `runSelfTest()` â\200\224 exercise the real film functions and return `{ passed, tests }`.\n\nThe self-test must genuinely verify deterministic reset, the exact 112000 ms duration, all 12 ordered sequence boundaries, exact seeking, `seekScene`, pause, replay, default autoplay with seed 1812, end-state reachability, and finite numeric state. After testing, restore the normal opening state: seed 1812, time 0, playing at 1Ã\227.\n\n## Technical constraints\n\n- Create exactly one complete `index.html` file.\n- Put all markup, styles, generated illustration, rendering, animation, editing, captions, controls, and logic in that file.\n- Do not load external libraries, fonts, images, SVG files, videos, audio, APIs, JSON, or other network assets.\n- Use only application-generated graphics and system fonts.\n- The film must open directly in a modern browser without a server or build step.\n- Do not embed copyrighted film stills, traced commercial illustrations, or external base64 assets.\n- Every visible control must work.\n\n## Quality bar\n\nThe result should feel like an independent animation studio, children\'s-book illustrator, cinematographer, production designer, editor, and creative coder made one cohesive miniature film together. Every sampled moment should look composed, but the work must become more powerful through time, continuity, and transformation.\n\nBuild the film directly without explaining the concept first. Run one concise validation pass across page load, autoplay, all 12 sequence boundaries, seeking, replay, responsive layout, deterministic snapshots, and the real self-test. Fix blocking failures, then keep the final response brief.\n\nWork only inside the current working directory. Do not edit files outside the current working directory.\n\nUse pnpm exclusively if dependency installation or package scripts are needed. Do not use npm.\nSet the browser title and the primary visible title to your model name, using only identity information already available to you. Add nothing else to either title.\nRun the relevant checks yourself. Do not edit files outside the current working directory.' 
